    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.transitive_reduction - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.transitive_reduction.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.transitive_reduction()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute the transitive reduction of a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.transitive_reduction()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute the transitive reduction of a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.transitive_reduction()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Compute the transitive reduction of a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the transitive reduction of a directed acyclic graph (DAG).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L74537-L74644){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.transitive_reduction()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Transitive reduction](../assets/graphs/transitive-reduction.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A DAG on A, B, C, D: the chain A to B to C to D plus three shortcut arrows A to C, B to D and A to D, in the accent color. The reduction drops the shortcuts, shown dashed, because the chain already reaches the same nodes.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe transitive reduction is the minimal subgraph that maintains the same reachability as the\noriginal graph. It removes redundant edges while preserving all path relationships. If\nthere's a path from `A` to `C` via `B`, the direct edge `A -> C` (if it exists) is redundant\nand will be removed.\n\nThis method only works on directed acyclic graphs (DAGs). Cyclic graphs do not have a unique\ntransitive reduction.\n\nTime complexity: `O(V × E)` where `V` is nodes and `E` is edges\nSpace complexity: `O(V + E)`\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   New directed graph representing the transitive reduction.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If called on an undirected graph or a graph containing cycles.\n:::\n\n## Common Use Cases {.doc-common-use-cases}\n\n- simplifying dependency graphs to show only direct dependencies\n- reducing storage for reachability queries\n- visualizing the minimal structure of a DAG\n- cleaning up inferred relationships\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe transitive reduction of a DAG is *unique* (Aho, Garey, and Ullman, 1972): among\nall subgraphs with the same reachability, exactly one attains the minimum edge\ncount, and this call returns that graph. Uniqueness fails for cyclic graphs (any\nedge on a cycle can be removed without losing reachability), so the implementation\nraises `ValueError` on cyclic input rather than picking an arbitrary reduction. The\nconstruction runs in $O(V \\cdot E)$ time and $O(V + E)$ space. Reductions are\nespecially useful for visualizing the essential dependency structure of a DAG\nwithout the visual clutter of edges implied by transitivity.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~transitive_closure`) \n\n:   Every edge with the same reachability.\n\n[](`~topological_sort`) \n\n:   Order the nodes so that every edge points forward.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe reduction drops the arrow from `A` to `C`, since `A` already reaches `C` through `B`:\n\n::: {#5b99b443 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"C\")], directed=True\n)\nreduced = graph.transitive_reduction()\n\nreduced.count_edges(), reduced.has_edge(\"A\", \"C\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(2, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A redundant prerequisite\n\n`courses()` holds the courses of a made-up computer science program, with an arrow from\neach course to every course that requires it. CS201 lists CS101 as a prerequisite, but it\nalso requires CS102, which requires CS101. The reduction drops that one arrow, and the\norder in which courses can be taken doesn't change:\n\n::: {#74dbcf63 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncourses = ec.courses()\nreduced = courses.transitive_reduction()\n\n(\n    courses.count_edges(),\n    reduced.count_edges(),\n    reduced.has_edge(\"CS101\", \"CS201\"),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(19, 18, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#9581888e .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nstages = dict(enumerate(courses.topological_generations()))\n\ncourses.layout_multipartite(subset_key=stages).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight=[(\"CS101\", \"CS201\")],\n    label_by=\"label\", height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"420\" viewBox=\"3.2 14.0 686.7 412.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<